history.idea:
Replace the hard p>=0.5 filter with rank-and-fill to exactly T tokens with no cycling, so the model never sees a small set repeated.

1. Dedupe: drop exact duplicates only (hash of whitespace-normalised, lowercased text), keeping the first occurrence.

2. Hard-drop only obvious junk:
- docs under 200 characters;
- docs with zlib compression ratio below 0.25 (repetitive spam);
- docs with under 60% alphabetic-or-space characters (code, HTML);
- word-shuffled docs: over the first 400 whitespace tokens, compute the fraction of bigrams found in the bigram set of target_sample plus the rate of common function bigrams ('of the', 'in the', 'to the', ', and'). Drop a doc only if its function-bigram rate is below 25% of the median rate in target_sample.

3. Domain score with no classifier trained on contaminated negatives: build smoothed unigram word log-probabilities from target_sample and from the whole pool. Score each doc by the mean per-token log-likelihood ratio (target vs pool) over its first 1000 tokens. Add a bonus of +1.0 if the doc starts with the WikiText header pattern '= Title =' (regex ^\s*= [^=\n]+ =\s*\n) or contains ' @-@ '.

4. Selection: sort the surviving docs by score, descending. Count tokens with tiktoken gpt2 (encode_ordinary_batch) and take docs from the top until the cumulative count reaches T. This fills with the most target-like clean prose and adds no repeats. Allow cycling only if all survivors together are under T.

5. Order: shuffle the selected list with CURATE_SEED.

Keep dedupe, junk filters, order test, LLR scoring and the seeded global shuffle unchanged. Change only how the T-token budget is filled: let clean target-format docs appear twice before any off-domain filler is used.

1. After scoring, split the survivors into:
- core: docs with a WikiText marker (matches the '= Title =' header regex or contains ' @-@ ');
- filler: all other survivors.
Sort each group by descending LLR score. Count gpt2 tokens for all core docs with encode_ordinary_batch, and for filler only as needed, keeping the existing time-budget fallback.

2. Build the selection by cumulative token count, stopping as soon as the total reaches T:
- a. add every core doc once, in score order;
- b. if still under T, add a second copy of the core docs, in score order (at most 2 copies of any doc);
- c. if still under T, add filler docs in descending score order, one copy each;
- d. if still under T, return what there is and let the trainer cycle.

3. Shuffle the final list (with its repeats) once with random.Random(CURATE_SEED), as now.

4. Print or log core_tokens / T, the number of second copies used and the filler tokens used, so the composition can be checked.
